1763 0 obj
<<
/ActualText (Erving Goffman\031s Behavior in Public Places is useful for understanding this kind of situation. Goffman examines forms of interaction that take place between people who are physically co-present, including \034unfocused interaction\035, where individuals share a situation without necessarily entering into direct communication \(Goffman, 1963, pp. 17\02324\). This is close to the social condition I want the installation to create. The participants do not need to speak, cooperate or even consciously perform together; nevertheless, their movements and sounds become part of the same perceptual environment.\n\nEdward T. Hall\031s concept of proxemics also provides a useful framework. In The Hidden Dimension, Hall describes interpersonal distance and the way spatial organisation influences social relations and bodily behaviour. The three handles create three temporary positions within the same structure, but these positions are not experienced identically. Height, reach, posture and habitual interpersonal distance influence how participants stand, how strongly they pull, and how closely they position themselves to others. Rather than treating these differences simply as noise within the sensing system, I became interested in allowing bodily difference to remain visible and audible.)
/K [1]
/S /Span
/Type /StructElem
/P 1764 0 R
/Pg 1392 0 R
>>
endobj
