1769 0 obj
<<
/ActualText (Sound supports this structure because it is simultaneously individual and spatial. Brandon LaBelle\031s writing on sound and everyday acoustic environments helped me think about listening as relational rather than purely representational. A sound can originate from one action while occupying a space shared with several people. Sounds can overlap, interfere and become perceptible without requiring participants to look directly at one another \(Brandon LaBelle, 2019, pp. xi\023xviii\). In the installation, sound therefore operates not simply as feedback from a sensor, but as one of the means through which another participant becomes present.\nPublic transport remains important within this framework, but its role has also changed. I no longer consider the work to be primarily about a bus journey. Instead, the suspended transport handle acts as a familiar spatial cue. It quickly suggests a condition in which strangers temporarily occupy the same infrastructure, retain their individual positions, and are free to enter and leave. This allows me to construct a minimal form of public space without attempting to reproduce an entire vehicle.)
/K [4]
/S /Span
/Type /StructElem
/P 1770 0 R
/Pg 1392 0 R
>>
endobj
