/ActualText (The mapping of resistance changes due to stretching and the sound synthesis were developed in Pure Data running on a Bela board, with each handrail assigned its own input channel. I first smoothed the sensor readings and then mapped them to sound parameters. Calibration for each sensor was performed experimentally, collecting data on resting value ranges, tension value ranges, and post-release value ranges. For example, for the first channel, I recorded resting values approximately between 0.500\0230.507, tension values around 0.439\0230.461, and post-release values roughly 0.487\0230.496. Each physical sensor required individual calibration rather than using a shared, universal threshold.\nDuring development I tested different approaches, including more layered and complex timbral behaviours. However, these often moved the work too far towards a descriptive or overly musical sound. In the final version, I chose to retain sine-wave-based sound generation. This created a simpler and more direct relationship between pulling force and sonic output, while also maintaining a coherent sonic language across the three handles.\nThis decision was also conceptual. I did not want the sound to overdetermine the interpretation of the work or become too illustrative. The relative sparseness of the sine-wave output allowed the sound to function more as a marker of bodily presence and tension within a shared space.)
/K [4]
/S /Span
