/ActualText (This project became considerably more technically and materially demanding than I had initially expected. I entered the project with previous experience in Arduino and Max/MSP, but had not previously worked with e-textiles, Bela, Pure Data or sound installation. As a result, much of the development process involved learning new tools while simultaneously attempting to make them stable enough for exhibition.\nOne of the clearest lessons was the difference between a functioning prototype and a reliable installation. Early tests proved relatively quickly that stretching a sensor could be translated into sound, and I was able to run an early Bela prototype independently from the laptop. My journal records this transition from initial sensing tests to standalone operation and then to multi-sensor calibration.  However, expanding the system to three physical interfaces introduced problems that were difficult to anticipate at prototype scale: different sensor ranges, signal drift, mechanical strain, cable abrasion, loose breadboard connections and eventually sensor breakage after wall-mounted testing.\nThese failures changed the way I approached making. Instead of treating technical problems as interruptions to the \034real\035 artwork, I gradually understood them as part of the design process. Replacing unstable handmade textile sensors, soldering the final circuit, adding mechanical limiters and protecting the sensor where it passed through the wooden structure were all responses to what the material system revealed through use. The final form was therefore produced through repeated cycles of testing, failure and modification rather than through the execution of a fixed plan.\nThe main weakness of the final version is the sound design. I experimented with more complex mappings and timbres, but eventually returned to relatively simple sine-wave voices in order to prioritise interaction reliability. While this produced a clear distinction between the three handles, I do not yet feel that the sonic language has developed to the same degree as the physical and conceptual structure of the installation. This is an unresolved part of the work rather than a decision I consider fully complete.)
