4209 0 obj
<<
/ActualText (During my Viva process, Milly recommended David Byrne\031s *Playing the Building*. In this installation, an interface resembling an organ activates motors, percussive elements, and air systems directly connected to architectural components\024such as metal beams, pipes, and ducts\024effectively transforming the building itself into a sounding body. This resonates with Jeremie\031s recommendation of artist Jean Tinguely, both involving physical sound generation. These references are particularly relevant to an unresolved question in my project: rather than merely using electronic synthesis as feedback, could the physical structure of the installation itself generate or shape sound?\nFuture iterations could explore transducers, contact microphones, or mechanically excited materials so that pulling a handle causes wooden supports, metal fittings, or tensioned elements to resonate. This would create a tighter connection between sound and the physical forces originally driving the project, potentially moving beyond the current version\031s relatively abstract sine wave output.\nI will also return to the relational dimension among the three participants. I don\031t necessarily need to reintroduce the initial concept of clearly defined \034successful\035 synchronized events. Instead, I\031m more interested in subtler forms of acoustic coupling: one participant\031s movement might slightly alter another\031s perception of resonance, spatial awareness, or acoustic conditions, while each person\031s voice remains distinct. In this way, future versions could further develop the conceptual shift emerging during the project\024from understanding the body as a medium for environmental forces, to viewing multiple bodies, objects, and sounds as temporarily co-constituting an environment.)
/K [3]
/S /Span
/Type /StructElem
/P 4210 0 R
/Pg 3940 0 R
>>
